ARM: dts: qcom: atv329q keep a placeholder Bluetooth address

The DTS carried local-bd-address = [ca 95 9b e3 00 02], i.e. an invented
02:00:E3:9B:95:CA, baked into every board built from this image while the real
address for this unit sat unused in persist/bluetooth/.bt_nv.bin. A first
attempt (this same patch, earlier revision) dropped the property outright, on
the reasoning that btqcomsmd implements hdev->set_bdaddr and the address could
be applied at runtime instead. Measured on hardware, that does not work:

  Bluetooth: hci0: Opcode 0x1001 failed: -110
  Bluetooth: hci0: command 0x1001 tx timeout

Without any address, hci0 never comes up at all (ACL MTU stays 0:0), so no
runtime tool -- btmgmt included -- can act on it afterwards. The WCNSS firmware
appears to need an address handed to it during bring-up to complete its own
init handshake; which address does not seem to matter, only that one exists.

So the property stays, but as a neutral placeholder shared by every board
rather than a per-board value. With it present, hci0 initialises normally
(ACL MTU 1024:7) and the real, per-board address is then applied at runtime:

  - S09identity (added alongside this patch) resolves it -- persist first,
    then derived from the eMMC CID if persist was wiped;
  - S35btaddr brings hci0 down, calls `btmgmt public-addr`, and brings it back
    up. Confirmed on hardware: hci0 comes back reporting the correct address.

That keeps a single image working across a fleet without baking any one board
real identity into the DTB, while working around the firmware requiring an
address to exist at all during init.

Verified: dtc returns 0 with no new warnings (23 before and after), and the
placeholder lands in the produced blob as local-bd-address = [00 00 00 00 00
02].

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-msm8909-geniatech-atv329q.dts b/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-msm8909-geniatech-atv329q.dts
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-msm8909-geniatech-atv329q.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-msm8909-geniatech-atv329q.dts
@@ -251,13 +251,35 @@
 	compatible = "qcom,wcn3620";
 };
 
-/* Bluetooth: WCNSS hosts BT on the same Pronto core as WiFi -> btqcomsmd bridges
- * HCI over the WCNSS_CTRL SMD channel (qcom,wcnss-bt node is in the .dtsi). The
- * WCNSS has no persistent BD address storage, so supply a fixed (locally
- * administered) one via the firmware property. Bytes are LITTLE-ENDIAN, i.e.
- * reversed vs the displayed address 02:00:E3:9B:95:CA. */
+/*
+ * Bluetooth: WCNSS hosts BT on the same Pronto core as WiFi, and btqcomsmd
+ * bridges HCI over the WCNSS_CTRL SMD channel (the qcom,wcnss-bt node lives in
+ * the .dtsi).
+ *
+ * local-bd-address is kept, but as a NEUTRAL PLACEHOLDER shared by every
+ * board, not a per-board value -- measured on hardware that it has to be
+ * present at all. Dropping the property outright (tried first) leaves the
+ * controller answering BDADDR_ANY, and the very first HCI command times out:
+ *
+ *   Bluetooth: hci0: Opcode 0x1001 failed: -110
+ *   Bluetooth: hci0: command 0x1001 tx timeout
+ *
+ * hci0 never comes up at all in that state (ACL MTU 0:0), so nothing --
+ * btmgmt included -- can act on it afterwards. The WCNSS firmware apparently
+ * needs an address handed to it during bring-up to complete its own init
+ * handshake; which address does not seem to matter, only that one exists.
+ *
+ * With the placeholder present, hci0 initialises normally (ACL MTU 1024:7),
+ * and S35btaddr then overrides it at runtime with the real address for this
+ * board, resolved by S09identity -- confirmed on hardware: down, btmgmt
+ * public-addr, up, and hci0 comes back reporting the correct address. That
+ * keeps a single image working across a fleet without baking any board real
+ * identity into the DTB.
+ *
+ * Bytes are LITTLE-ENDIAN, i.e. reversed vs the address as displayed.
+ */
 &wcnss_bt {
-	local-bd-address = [ ca 95 9b e3 00 02 ];
+	local-bd-address = [ 00 00 00 00 00 02 ];
 };
 
 /* USB OTG (micro-USB = device for adb/gadget ; USB-A = host when ID floats) */
